[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one issue, one fix queued. It is not one issue. The
reports were merged because their subject lines match ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)),
and nobody had to read the bodies. Read them, and they describe three problems. The parser fix
that is queued closes only the first.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. Three problems, each filed as its own ticket below. Do not close #4471 when the parser fix ships.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<SplitTable
  ticket={4471}
  tickets={{
    parser: 'https://github.com/elliotrushton/support-kb-4471/issues/1',
    hint: 'https://github.com/elliotrushton/support-kb-4471/issues/2',
    'day-shift': 'https://github.com/elliotrushton/support-kb-4471/issues/3',
  }}
/>

Every count is computed from `reports-4471.json` when the page renders, once per report id. The
export repeats some reports across its pages.

## The three problems

**`parser`: the shared parser reads dates month first.** Every tenant in #4471 writes the day
first. Any date whose day is 12 or lower comes out with day and month swapped: "Our 7 April
reminder went out on 4 July." It happens through the app, email and the `booking-v2` form. The
date the person meant reaches the parser, and the parser flips it. This is the fix that is queued.

**`hint`: the `booking-v3` form tells people to type the month first.** Its date field says
`MM/DD/YYYY`, so people type the month first. The form reads the tenant's way, day first. The date
is wrong before it reaches the parser, so the parser fix does not touch it. This covers only
reports sent through the form at tenants on `booking-v3`. The same tenants' app and email
reports are `parser`.

**`day-shift`: dates move by one day.** The month stays right. The day moves by one, or one
meeting lands on different days in different time zones: "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in
Toronto got Monday." That is time-zone handling, not date order. The desk already read these
reports as one-day moves (`desk-notes.json`), but `causeOf` sorts by tenant and channel only, so
the table on #4471 counts them under `parser`.

## How a report is placed

`problemOf` in `#lib/kb/split.ts`, in this order:

1. The desk read it as a one-day move: `day-shift`.
2. Otherwise the cause from `causes.ts`: `hint` when it came through a form that prints a
   month-first hint at a day-first tenant, else `parser`.

Intake's tag (`meta.intakeTag`) is not used. It is a guess from the reporter's browser: every
report from an `en-US` browser is tagged `hint`, and every other report is tagged `parser`,
whatever form the tenant runs and whichever way the report came in.

## Lessons

- A subject line says how the report felt to the person who wrote it, not what caused it. Three
  bugs can share one subject.
- Before you mark a big ticket known, read a sample of bodies from each tenant and channel.
- When the desk notes that some reports read differently, that is a new ticket, not a footnote.

